<article class="env-example" id="0DKP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DKP">Example <span data-tag="0DKP">76.52.2</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be an algebraic space of finite presentation over $k$ (in particular $X$ is quasi-compact). Then an object $E$ of $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$ is $k$-perfect if and only if it is bounded and pseudo-coherent (by definition), i.e., if and only if it is in $D^ b_{\textit{Coh}}(X)$ (by Derived Categories of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/08IK" data-tag="08IK">75.13.7</a>). Thus being relatively perfect does <strong>not</strong> mean “perfect on the fibres”. </p>
</article>